00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:55,000
I actually don't think you can talk even about how well the defile lending platforms

99
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:02,000
like Ave and Morfo have done without talking about the void that was left by Celsius and BlockFi

100
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:08,000
that had built what's probably the most straightforward use case of crypto,

101
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:14,000
which is I want to own Bitcoin and as much more Bitcoin as I can and less dollars,

102
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,000
but have spendable dollars against that Bitcoin.

103
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,000
I think it's that cut and simple.

104
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:24,000
And I think a big part of the success of those platforms was the fact that

105
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:29,000
Celsius and BlockFi and others not only went away,

106
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:36,000
but bred mistrust in those platforms and the defile platforms benefited from that.

107
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Yeah, and I think we can talk a lot about trust.

108
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:42,000
There's still a degree of trust me that goes into the platforms today.

109
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:48,000
And that's one of the things that we went into in this piece is rapidly these on chain platforms.

110
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,000
They're auditable.

111
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,000
You can see what's going on there,

112
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:56,000
but you almost need a PhD today to actually understand the risk that you're getting paid for.

113
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
And this is fundamental to the lending markets is it's not risk-free.

114
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
It's as close to the risk-free rate as you can get.

115
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:07,000
So you go back to Celsius and you have this huge need to unlock capital

116
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:11,000
that's just sitting there in these long positions that people want to use.

117
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,000
They want to unlock and for whatever reason,

118
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:19,000
whether it's taxes or whether it's just fundamentally that they want to hold on to it.

119
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Ideologically, they go to a lender to try to unlock the capital.

120
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,000
This use case is still pretty persistent.

121
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:30,000
And this is probably one of the most durable trades that we see on lending markets today.

122
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:31,000
I mean, it has to be.

123
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
It's extremely durable.

124
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,000
So like a great example of this is the Morpho integration with Coinbase.

125
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:42,000
So Morpho is integrated with Coinbase so you can borrow against your CBBTC,

126
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,000
which is Coinbase's RAP Bitcoin.

127
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:49,000
And this has just been growing exponentially since they started earlier this year.

128
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
It's over one and a half billion dollars of collateral in Bitcoin.

129
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,000
And this use case, when you look at the TVL chart in the drop-off after October,

130
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,000
this has not been hindered at all.

131
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:04,000
What you do see in the drop-off after October is another strategy that we saw come out recently,

132
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
which is this idea of looping.

133
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:10,000
For those who haven't listened to Henry and Corinne's podcast about the vaults,

134
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,000
this is extremely relevant for this.

135
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:17,000
But the general idea of looping is I have some yield-bearing token.

136
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,000
The yield might be coming from any of many sources.

137
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:28,000
Treasury yield, right-staking yield, basis trade through USDA, RWA lending, any of these.

138
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:34,000
And the idea or the realization that lending markets had is they can lend to these at a pretty high LTV.

139
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:38,000
And when you lend to them at a really high LTV, you can actually recycle that capital.

140
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:43,000
So you put in asset, you borrow against the stablecoin, you buy asset,

141
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,000
you put asset back in and you borrow against it.

142
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:47,000
And you continue to do this.

143
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:53,000
And what you can do is you can take a yield from maybe 10% on an unlevered basis

144
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,000
and lever it up to call it 25, 30%.

145
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:01,000
So this is what we really saw leading into October when we released this piece.

146
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
And admittedly, this was very scary.

147
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:07,000
And the reason this was very scary was because it's really two reasons.

148
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:15,000
One is, although crypto is extremely traceable, it's virtually impossible to identify how levered a system is.

149
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:21,000
It's very possible to go and do this looping on multiple platforms or from multiple accounts.

150
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
So this is not something that's easily traceable, not just to us,

151
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:29,000
but we spoke to all of the major lending protocols and all of them agreed that they don't know themselves.

152
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
It's not something that they can measure.

153
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:38,000
The second thing that's scary about this is the underlying increasingly went down the risk curve.

154
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:43,000
And what I mean by this is USDA is a relatively vanilla strategy.

155
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,000
Ever since I've been at L1D, we've been getting pitched basis trade funds.

156
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
And this was super popular in 2021.

157
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:54,000
When USDA came out, it just evaporated the basis trade fund pitch.

158
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
We stopped seeing them entirely.

159
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:01,000
And the reason being is because USDA essentially commodified that trade

160
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:07,000
and they wrapped it in a token and they made it so anybody can really access it with way less friction and they handle the infrastructure.

161
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,000
Now, this is something that a lot of crypto people are familiar with the risks on.

162
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
So they're comfortable underwriting it.

163
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:20,000
So when you see USDA being 20% of the Aave lending pool, you don't want the concentration risk perhaps,

164
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
but you're okay with the underlying risk.

165
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:28,000
What we saw after this is this implementation of this strategy across different vault strategies.

166
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:33,000
And by vault strategies, I really mean this idea of putting your tokens into a vault

167
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:36,000
and them running some sort of trading strategy beneath it.

168
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,000
There are levels of obfuscation that people use.

169
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:46,000
So going into the elixir event, what we discovered is essentially these vaults were just cross depositing into themselves.

170
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:52,000
And this is extremely dangerous because essentially you take one fund

171
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,000
and you're spreading the same amount of risk across all the other funds.

172
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:01,000
So this was a strategy that I think we really rightfully identified as not extremely durable.

173
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
It was very profitable for a lot of lending protocols.

174
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:07,000
It will continue to be a strategy that lending protocols enable,

175
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:13,000
but this is not what I would consider to be the bet on lending markets, so to speak.

176
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:17,000
I fully agree and you left me with a few different points.

177
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:21,000
I first off want to replay why we're having this conversation

178
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:27,000
and then some of the key points you brought up, which were you and I as long-term investors

179
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:31,000
in the business of finding what the good and lasting business models are

180
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:35,000
and we're looking at lending in crypto as one case of those

181
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,000
because what we think is a market gap that'll grow

182
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:44,000
and because of what we think could be strengths of why that business model is durable, which you made points on.

183
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
And then in examining those lending protocols,

184
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:51,000
trying to pick out what's important and vetting the risks to your point

185
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:54,000
and that you see these metrics like total value locked inflecting up,

186
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,000
but you don't know the sense of how much leverage is in the system.

187
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:03,000
And what I've thought is a very funny characteristic of the current crypto market

188
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:08,000
where so long composability or all the apps working well together

189
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
was such a North Star that people wanted.

